paths: file:std/aggregator.ssc file:examples/std-aggregator.ssc file:specs/aggregation-algebra.md file:BACKLOG.md file:tests/conformance/std-aggregator.ssc file:tests/conformance/expected/std-aggregator.txt
scope: |
  Finish §5 of specs/aggregation-algebra.md: first/last, the one item left after the
  aggregator-canonical-exact claim (min/max, variance/stddev).

  Design decision (was open, deciding now): generalize min/max into a keyed MinByAgg[A, K]/
  MaxByAgg[A, K] (explicit Order[K] + a key: A => K function, additive - MinAgg/MaxAgg stay as
  they are, not refactored to be defined in terms of these, to avoid touching already-shipped/
  tested code for no behavioral gain). first/last are then MinByAgg/MaxByAgg keyed on a caller-
  attached index: the caller runs xs.zipWithIndex (-> List[(A, Int)], element first per this
  codebase's existing convention) before folding, and firstAgg[A]()/lastAgg[A]() do
  MinByAgg/MaxByAgg on the index, mapped back to just the element. This is what makes it a real
  Aggregator usable in parallel/combine (the ordering key travels WITH the value instead of being
  implicit in fold order), matching the spec's own warning ("do not merge unordered partitions
  naively").

  Also: verify variance/min/max/mean interop is undisturbed (regression), and don't touch
  std/order.ssc further - it's stable now.
next: implement MinByAgg/MaxByAgg + firstAgg/lastAgg, test, land
